---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Why Eddies Games Exists | Eddies Games"
  description="Why Eddies Games focuses on small browser games, finished projects, and learning by actually building things."
>
  <article class="article">
    <header class="article-header">
      <p class="eyebrow">Behind the Games</p>

      <h1>Why Eddies Games Exists</h1>

      <p class="dek">
        Small games, finished projects, and learning by actually building things.
      </p>

      <p class="meta">September 16, 2026</p>
    </header>

    <div class="article-body">
      <p>
        Eddies Games exists for a pretty simple reason: I like making games, and
        I like finishing them.
      </p>

      <p>That second part matters more than it sounds.</p>

      <p>
        It is very easy to come up with a huge game idea. Open-world this,
        multiplayer that, fifty systems talking to another fifty systems, and
        before long you have designed something that would keep a studio busy
        for three years.
      </p>

      <p>That is not what I wanted this site to become.</p>

      <h2>Small Games Can Still Be Good Games</h2>

      <p>
        The goal here is not to build the biggest games possible. It is to build
        games that are fun, easy to understand, quick to load, and actually get
        finished.
      </p>

      <p>
        Some are puzzle games. Some are arcade-style games. Some are daily
        challenges. Some are simply ideas that sounded interesting enough to
        turn into something playable.
      </p>

      <p>
        The important part is that they move from idea, to prototype, to
        finished game.
      </p>

      <h2>Building Is How I Learn</h2>

      <p>
        I have always learned more by making something than by endlessly reading
        about how something is supposed to be made.
      </p>

      <p>
        A game gives you plenty of opportunities to find out what you do not
        know.
      </p>

      <p>
        Controls feel wrong. A puzzle rule makes perfect sense in your head but
        confuses everyone else. A layout looks great on a desktop and then turns
        into modern art on a phone. A mechanic technically works but simply does
        not feel good.
      </p>

      <p>Then you fix it.</p>

      <p>
        Or occasionally stare at it for a while and wonder why it ever seemed
        like a good idea in the first place.
      </p>

      <h2>Finishing Matters</h2>

      <p>
        Every completed game teaches me something I can carry into the next one.
        That is one of the main ideas behind Eddies Games.
      </p>

      <p>
        Instead of waiting until I somehow know everything required to make the
        perfect game, I would rather build one, finish it, learn from it, and
        make the next one better.
      </p>

      <p>
        That also means the games on this site may evolve. I might improve the
        interface, adjust difficulty, change how feedback works, or revisit a
        mechanic after seeing it used in the real world.
      </p>

      <p>
        A finished game does not have to mean a game that can never change.
      </p>

      <h2>Why Browser Games?</h2>

      <p>Browser games fit this approach particularly well.</p>

      <p>
        There is no installer, no account requirement, and no twenty-minute
        download before someone can find out whether they actually like the
        game.
      </p>

      <p>You open the page and play.</p>

      <p>
        That also makes the site a useful place for experimenting with ideas.
        Some games may remain small browser games forever. Others may eventually
        lead to larger projects or versions on other platforms.
      </p>

      <h2>Behind the Games</h2>

      <p>
        This section of the site exists because the finished game is only part
        of the story.
      </p>

      <p>
        There are usually plenty of decisions, mistakes, rewrites, and strange
        little problems hiding behind something that looks simple when you play
        it.
      </p>

      <p>
        So these articles will cover where game ideas came from, why certain
        mechanics work the way they do, what changed during development, what
        failed, and what I learned while building them.
      </p>

      <p>
        Not tutorials pretending everything worked perfectly the first time.
      </p>

      <p>Because it usually did not.</p>

      <h2>So What Is Eddies Games?</h2>

      <p>It is a collection of games I wanted to make.</p>

      <p>
        It is also a running record of learning by building, experimenting, and
        finishing things.
      </p>

      <p>
        Some ideas will work better than others. Some will probably get rebuilt.
        A few may turn out to have been terrible ideas from the start.
      </p>

      <p>That is part of the fun.</p>

      <p>Build it. Play it. Learn something. Make the next one.</p>
    </div>

    <footer class="article-footer">
      <a href="/articles" class="back">← Behind the Games</a>
      <a href="/" class="cta">Play the games →</a>
    </footer>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .article {
    max-width: 780px;
    margin: 0 auto;
  }

  .article-header {
    margin-bottom: 36px;
  }

  .eyebrow {
    margin: 0 0 8px;
    color: var(--ember);
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .article-header h1 {
    margin: 0 0 12px;
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3rem);
    line-height: 1.1;
  }

  .dek {
    margin: 0 0 12px;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .meta {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 0.85rem;
  }

  .article-body {
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    line-height: 1.75;
  }

  .article-body p {
    margin: 0 0 20px;
  }

  .article-body h2 {
    margin: 42px 0 14px;
    font-size: 1.5rem;
  }

  .article-footer {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 20px;
    margin-top: 48px;
    padding-top: 24px;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .cta {
    color: var(--ember);
    font-weight: 700;
  }

  .back {
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .back:hover,
  .cta:hover {
    color: var(--text);
  }

  @media (max-width: 600px) {
    .article-footer {
      flex-direction: column;
    }
  }
</style>
